{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass at evening.</b> At the very end of the day itself." "<b>And the quail came up.</b> Perhaps it came up from the direction of the sea." "<b>A layer of dew.</b> Like \"Who can lay flat the bottles of heaven?\" (Job 38:37) — the meaning is descent, and elsewhere it is written \"and when the dew fell\" (Numbers 11:9). There [the author] mentions a creature [?] who said that the manna is what is called in Persian תרנגבי\"ן, in Arabic מ\"ן, and in the foreign tongue מנ\"א, for many difficulties stand against him: first, that it does not fall today in the wilderness of Sinai, for the mountain is known; and I myself have seen something resembling manna in the kingdom of al-Ṣakhīr and it falls in Nisan and Iyyar and not in other months; and further, if you set it in the sun it does not melt; and further, at night it does not stink; and further, it is not hard, and there is no need for a person to pound it in a mortar to make cakes from it; and further, when placed on the tongue it melts; and further, it does not satisfy so as to produce good blood, but rather enters into remedies; and further, that on the sixth day a double portion fell; and further, that it did not fall on the Sabbath; and further, that it fell wherever they encamped; and further, that it crossed the Jordan with them and did not cease until mid-Nisan, according to the plain sense."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {2 "The word ירקב appears to be corrupt or a foreign name; translated hyper-literally as 'a creature' to preserve the difficulty without resolving it. The phrase ידוכנו אדם reflects a likely misdivision of ידוכנו דך; translated as 'a person to pound it.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:09.742005757Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}